      <h2>Why Do These Specific States Rank at the Extremes?</h2>

      <h3>The 3 Most Expensive States: Florida, Michigan & Louisiana</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Florida ($3,640/yr avg):</strong> Florida policyholders face a confluence of risk drivers: a 20.4% uninsured driver rate, mandatory PIP coverage, high severe convective weather/hurricane frequency, and extensive third-party litigation against carriers.</li>
        <li><strong>Michigan ($3,480/yr avg):</strong> Despite recent tort reforms, Michigan remains exceptionally expensive due to historical unlimited personal injury lifetime medical benefits and Michigan Catastrophic Claims Association (MCCA) statutory assessments on every registered vehicle.</li>
        <li><strong>Louisiana ($3,320/yr avg):</strong> Louisiana’s "direct action statute" allows plaintiffs to sue insurance companies directly rather than individual drivers, creating high lawsuit frequency and elevated bodily injury loss costs.</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>The 3 Cheapest States: Maine, Vermont & Idaho</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Maine ($1,170/yr avg):</strong> Maine boasts one of the lowest uninsured driver rates in the nation (under 4.5%), minimal urban density, low auto theft statistics, and stable tort liability rules.</li>
        <li><strong>Vermont ($1,210/yr avg):</strong> Rural driving conditions, responsible demographic profiles, and low per-capita litigation keep claim loss ratios exceptionally mild for local carriers.</li>
        <li><strong>Idaho ($1,265/yr avg):</strong> Idaho features spacious interstate infrastructure, lower density metropolitan areas, and aggressive state law enforcement against uninsured motorists.</li>
      </ul>

